@@@ changelog
# Changelog

Format: [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/), versioning: [SemVer](https://semver.org/).
The file format has its own version (`%KAPU 1.3`, see `SPEC-KAPU.md`); the tools below are versioned separately.

## [Unreleased]

## [0.5.0] - 2026-10-28

Specification v1.3: a file whose `#VEC` holds 4- or 8-bit integers is format 1.3, every other file stays 1.2. Plan v3 (D168-D172): the bundled vector model is now `multilingual-e5-small`, a contextual encoder run in plain Rust (`bench/RESULTS.md` chapter 13, `bench/results/2026-10-28-plan-v3.json`, the results page with a new section and its PDFs). There is no 0.4.0.

### Changed
- The bundled vector model is `multilingual-e5-small` in its int8 form (`models/multilingual-e5-small-q8`, 135 MB) instead of `potion-multilingual-128M` (D172). The release workflow downloads the int8 ONNX file at a fixed commit, checks its SHA-256, converts it with `tools/onnx_to_kapu.py` and checks the converted weights, so every platform has the same `embhash`; it also runs the encoder's golden test on Windows, Linux and macOS.
- A file built with the static model of the earlier releases keeps its vector search as long as that model is installed (the installers leave it in place); otherwise its search is BM25 only, and the reply says that `kapu build` rebuilds it with the new model. `kapu_write` on such a file rebuilds it with the bundled model instead of dropping its vectors. A first build with the new model takes minutes on a large file (Kubernetes 15 minutes, MDN 50 minutes on 12 threads), a rebuild only computes new text.
- The trade, measured and accepted (D171, D172): room-level recall@10 +0.062 and MRR@10 +0.028 on the test part of seven question sets; Hungarian questions on English skills lose MRR (0.49 to 0.44), a small file is searched about 35 ms slower (the query's vector, release build), a large one faster (MDN 408 to 320 ms), and files are 9 to 16% larger.

### Added
- A contextual vector model (D171): `multilingual-e5-small` in the int8 form of ONNX Runtime's dynamic quantization, run in plain Rust (`kapu --model <folder>`), with no new dependency and no download at run time. `tools/onnx_to_kapu.py` makes the model folder from the ONNX file. Every product is exact in integers and every float sum has a fixed order, so a vector has the same bits on SSE2, AVX2 and AVX-512 builds. Its windows are stored whole in `#VEC`, 4 bits a component, and scored without read-time rescoring. On the test part of seven question sets, against the bundled model: room-level recall@10 +0.062 (95% interval +0.035 to +0.090), MRR@10 +0.028; Hungarian questions on English skills lose MRR (0.49 to 0.44), a small file is searched 12 to 35 ms slower (the query's vector), MDN faster (408 to 156 ms), and the file is 9 to 16% larger.
- A contextual model builds on every core, the longest windows first, and keeps the windows it computed in a log in the cache folder, so an interrupted build goes on where it stopped and a rebuild computes only new windows. `kapu` reports the progress. A window that cannot be computed is warning W009, not a failed build.
- Ranking switches for measurement, off by default: passage BM25 with heading prefix, room-level fusion and vector prefix (D168), pseudo-relevance feedback (D169).

### Fixed
- Importing a folder no longer stops at a long file name or a control character in a file (F1, F2).

## [0.3.0] - 2026-10-27

Specification v1.2.2; the file format stays 1.2. Phases 1 to 3 of plan v2: every ranking change was measured as a switch on a fixed dev/test split of the questions, and only what passed the M0 rule became a default (`bench/RESULTS.md` chapter 12, `bench/results/2026-10-27-plan-v2.json`).

### Changed
- The README starts with a 30-second quickstart (install, pack, connect), then a short before-and-after of the measurements; the full benchmark tables are folded, and how it works and the desktop window come before the comparisons.
- `kapu_search` gives one hit per room (D158, J1): the best door stays the hit, and a line under it names the room's other matching sections (`+3 in this room: syntax, examples, ...`). On the test part of seven question sets: room-level recall@10 +0.022 (95% interval +0.010 to +0.035), MRR@10 +0.011, no set worse, search time unchanged. The `kapu bench` hybrid ranking collapses the same way, and `kapu search` names up to five other matching sections under a hit (all in its JSON, `also`). The cost: when a whole room outranks its sections, the hit is the room and the answering section is only named under it (A57).
- `kapu_ask` opens the start of the best door of each of the first 5 rooms, the budget shared, instead of the best hit up to its whole budget (D159, J12). The expected room is in the reply for 42% instead of 15% of the Rust book questions, 39% instead of 17% on Kubernetes and 22% instead of 12% on MDN, in about 1,130 instead of 1,800 tokens.
- The float windows the vector rescoring keeps in memory are also bounded by their number, 250,000 (D157): on an MDN-sized file the memory bounded by doors alone grew past 20 GB in a long run.

### Added
- One-line installs from the release archives, no Rust or Python needed: `install.sh` (Linux x64, macOS arm64) and `install.ps1` (Windows x64) download the archive of the latest release, check its SHA-256 and put `kapu`, `kapu-mcp` and `kapu-gui` with the embedding model under the user's folder. The release workflow writes a `.sha256` file next to each archive, and the archives include `examples/kapu-docs.kapu`.
- The specification in English (`SPEC-KAPU.md`); the Hungarian original is `SPEC-KAPU.hu.md`. The translation also fixed one cross-reference (the standard guide is FR-17, not FR-15). The example `examples/kapu-spec.kapu` is now the English text.
- `docs/assets/social-preview.png`, the repository's preview image for links (`tools/make_social.py`).
- The ablation framework (D156): `RankOptions` with every switch of plan v2 (stemming, separate average lengths, parent score, BM25 hits in the rescoring, weighted, query-dependent and score-based fusion, collapse, short-door cut-off, a graph step), off by default; off, the ranking is bit for bit that of 0.2.1 (`tests/vectors/rankings-0.2.1.tsv`). `kapu bench --config <json>` runs one configuration or a matrix and `--rankings` writes every question's rankings; `kapu-mcp --rank <json>` runs the server with them.
- Study tools: `split_questions.py` (the fixed 40/60 dev/test split, `bench/splits/`), `ablation.py` (metrics, paired bootstrap, error classes, the M0 rule), `ask_eval.py`, `neighbour_follow.py`, `group_quality.py`, `title_weight.py`, `oracle_eval.py` (oracle, KAPU and closed-book answers, and abstention), `collect_v2.py`, `v2_tables.py`; `chunk_baseline.py` keeps per-question ranks and adds a chunk hybrid.
- A new measurement corpus: public PLC manuals of six vendors (623 pages, local only), with 72 answerable, 24 absent and 12 two-page questions. With the KAPU tools Claude Sonnet answered 92% correctly (97% with the right page in the prompt, 28% alone) and said "not found" for 23 of 24 absent questions, where alone it made up 11 answers (N1, N4).
- The tool-list test prices the six tools (K-01: at most 800 tokens; now 799).

### Measured and not changed
- Separate average lengths for rooms and sections (J2a), a share of the room's score for its sections (J2b), the first 50 BM25 hits in the vector rescoring (J4a), weighted, query-dependent and min-max fusion (J4b-J4d), no own hits for sections under 40 tokens (J10), a graph step from the hits (J9), stemming with one stemmer or none, and title weights 1, 2 and 5: none passed the rule; they stay as switches, off.

## [0.2.1] - 2026-10-26

Specification v1.2.1; the file format stays 1.2.

### Changed
- `kapu_map kind=` and `kapu map --kind` give the kind's catalog (D154): one line per room with its price and the start of its description, so an agent can choose skills from one reply; a skill's reference rooms are folded into the skill (`+5 rooms`). Its default budget is 4000 tokens, and the first map names the catalog of the most common kind.
- `kapu_open` with several ids shares the budget evenly (D155), so one call shows the start of every door; without `max_tokens` each door gets 2000 tokens, for up to five doors.

### Added
- A new app icon from the artwork of 2026-10-26: the arched gate and centred map lines in a framed dark tile (`tools/make_icons.py`): the window's icons, the interface, the README logo and the results page.
- The results page as PDF, in English and Hungarian (`docs/results/kapu-measurements-{en,hu}.pdf`, `tools/study/results_pdf.py`).
- The skill-finding measurement (D153): `tools/study/skill_pool.py`, `skill_eval.py` and `skill_tasks.json`, results in `bench/RESULTS.md` chapter 11 and on the results page.

## [0.2.0] - 2026-10-26

Specification v1.2 and file format 1.2, from the two review records of 2026-10-26. KAPU is now aimed at two cases: a personal knowledge base and memory for coding agents on any platform, and knowledge for local models with a small context.

### Added
- Search vectors in the file (D143): an optional `#VEC` section holds the sign bits of every window vector, 43 base64 characters per window. The sign bits pick 200 candidates and the model's float windows order them, so the first search needs no cache, works on any machine and ranks as the float vectors did: room-level recall@10, hybrid, Rust book 0.485 (same), Kubernetes docs 0.458 (same), MDN 0.271 to 0.278, the English skills corpus 0.948 to 0.961. The file grows by 1.7-3.3% (Rust book 1.7%, Kubernetes docs 2.2%, the English skills corpus 2.0%, MDN 3.3%); float or int8 vectors would have added 13-25%. Search takes a little longer for the rescoring (Kubernetes docs 33 to 48 ms, MDN 198 to 277 ms, median). The `dim=` of a `#VEC` section is at most 4096 (D151, found by the new `vec` fuzz target: a file could make the reader allocate a scoring table of any size).
- `kind:` on a room (D144): `skill`, `project`, `pattern` or `note` (others allowed with warning W006), in the room's head. The map (`kapu_map kind=`, `kapu map --kind`) and the search (`kind`) filter by it, and the totals line counts the kinds (`kinds=skill:30`).
- Project cards (D144): `kapu new --kind project` and `kapu template --kind project` give a card with Goal, Period, Stack, Key decisions, What worked, What did not and Where the code is; W007 warns above 600 tokens.
- `kapu_ask` (D146): search and open in one call, within `max_tokens` (default 2000): the best door (or its first part), more whole doors if they fit, and the other matches.
- `kapu import --skills <folder> <file>` (D147): every `SKILL.md` folder is a room with the skill's name as title and its description as the `>` line, `kind: skill`, `has_scripts: yes` and the scripts as code blocks; reference Markdown files are rooms of their own, linked from the skill. Nothing is run.
- A `max_tokens` reply budget on every reading tool (D145): `kapu_map` 1000, `kapu_search` 600, `kapu_describe`, `kapu_open` and `kapu_ask` 2000 by default; a reply never goes over it.
- The first line of every MCP reply is its own price: `reply: 523 tokens` (D145).
- `kapu` warns (W008) when a file is above 50 MiB, GitHub's warning size (D148).
- A CI job that builds and tests with the declared minimum Rust (D142).
- `tests/compat/minta-1.1.kapu`, a format 1.1 file the reader must keep reading.
- `tools/study/measure_v12.py` (reply budgets, one-call answers) and `tools/study/translate_hu.py` (the Hungarian questions as an agent writes them in English).
- `examples/kapu-docs.kapu`: this documentation as a KAPU file, the quickstart example (`tools/make_examples.py`).
- The results page `docs/results/index.html` (English and Hungarian, written by `tools/study/results_page.py` from `bench/results/`) and the animated terminal demo `docs/assets/demo.svg` (`tools/make_demo_svg.py`).
- Real-corpus measurements: `bench/RESULTS.md`, `bench/results/`.
- `kapu bench --json`: room-level recall@5/10 and MRR@10, where any door of the expected room is a hit (D130).
- Study pilot tools in `tools/study/`: corpus preparation from public documentation, Stack Overflow questions with a gold page, chunk-RAG baselines, scale probe, logged file-search, llms.txt and RAG tools for agents.
- Tools: `tools/collect_skills.py`, `tools/mcp_probe.py`, `tools/kapu_mcp_call.py`, `tools/mcp_eval.py`.


### Changed
- The minimum Rust is 1.90 (`rust-version`, measured: Tauri needs 1.90), not 1.99. `rust-toolchain.toml` asks for `stable`; CI and the release workflow pin 1.99.1 (D142).
- `kapu_open`'s `max_tokens` is the budget of the whole reply (default 2000), not the part size; a part is cut so the reply fits, and `part=N` of a later call cuts at the same places (D145).
- `kapu_search`'s door-price filter is `max_price` (`--max-price` on the command line; `--max-tokens` still works there); `max_tokens` is the reply budget (default 600). The `excerpts` switch is gone: every hit has its excerpt line, at most 100 characters instead of 160 (D145).
- The tool list costs 797 tokens instead of 1174, with six tools instead of five: shorter descriptions, compact input schemas, and the std-1 guide only in the server's instructions and the first map (D145).
- A map is flat (every door line without a group) up to 800 tokens instead of 2000 (D145).
- The specification starts with the two use cases and what KAPU is not for; the desktop window is in scope (J14).
- Search by meaning uses one vector per window of a door's text (at most 512 tokens, overlapping by an eighth); the best window counts (D141). Room-level recall@10 on real questions, hybrid: Rust book 0.46 to 0.49, Kubernetes docs 0.44 to 0.46; on MDN, with its many short sections, 0.28 to 0.27. The vector cache (`.vecs2`) keeps the windows of unchanged doors after a write, and `kapu import`, `build`, `add` and `rm` compute them right away.
- File format 1.1 (specification v1.1, D140): an `#INDEX` posting is `<gap>:<tf>`, the gap between the doors' line numbers in `#DOCS`, instead of the door id. Files are about 4.5 times smaller (Kubernetes docs: 117 MB to 26 MB, MDN: 542 MB to 128 MB) and the 13-million-token MDN corpus builds within the 1 MiB line limit. Format 1.0 files are still read; `kapu build` rewrites them as 1.1.
- Specification v1.0.5 (D139): one call parses and checks the file once, a term's `#INDEX` line is found by binary search, and the MCP server keeps a checked copy of an unchanged file. Search on the Kubernetes docs: 657 ms to 19 ms (median), on MDN 3.2 s to 158 ms; opening a door: 216 ms to 5 ms.
- Specification v1.0.4 (D131-D138): group names leave out parameter-table words; the search excerpt prefers rarer query words; the map's totals line names the content language (`lang=`) and the guide asks for queries in that language; at most 10 content-scan lines on the map (`scan=true` lists all); `kapu_open` serves long doors in parts (`max_tokens`, `part`); the MCP server keeps unchanged files and door vectors in memory.
- Specification v1.0.2: in-page anchors link to the room's own sections and no longer warn (D123); group names leave out stop words (D121).
- `kapu-mcp`: tool annotations, at most 50 hits, a next step in every error (D120); an excerpt line under each search hit (D124); a group lists its rooms only and `room=` lists one room's sub-doors (D125); at most 8 neighbours per opened door (D126); a totals line on the map (D127).


### Removed
- The internal planning documents (status log, decision log, open questions, milestone plan, research plans, early concept archive) left the repository; decisions are still cited by their D-numbers in the specification and the code.


### Fixed
- The MCP server runs every call on a blocking thread, so a large file no longer stalls the stdio loop (A24, D149).
- Invisible characters (U+200B-U+200D, U+2060, U+FEFF) in a room's title or first sentence no longer produce a file the reader rejects (D135).
- `kapu ... | head` no longer panics when the reader closes the pipe early (D138).
- The S5 content scan no longer matches a command name inside another word (`async`, `confirm`) (D122).


## [0.1.0] - 2026-10-23

First portfolio release. Specification v1.0.1, milestones M0-M7 and M10 done, M8 and M9 in part.

### Added
- `kapu-core`: reader, validator (error codes E001-E012), builder with BM25 index, token prices (o200k_base), YAKE tags, BLAKE3 fingerprints, graph (`link`, `term`, `sim` edges), Leiden groups and hybrid search (BM25 + static embeddings, RRF).
- `kapu` command line tool: `new`, `import`, `export`, `add`, `rm`, `build`, `map`, `search`, `describe`, `open`, `validate`, `bench`; every command with `--json`.
- `kapu-mcp`: MCP server over stdio with `kapu_map`, `kapu_search`, `kapu_describe`, `kapu_open`, `kapu_write`, the std-1 guide and the content scan (FR-19).
- `kapu-gui`: desktop window (Tauri 2) with the map and token prices, room reader, search with a token budget, graph view and file checks.
- Demo file `examples/kapu-spec.kapu`: the specification packed as a KAPU file.
- Author signature `psz // v0.1.0`: last line of `kapu --help`, app info card on `--version` and in the window.
- Packed vector model: `potion-multilingual-128M` in int8 (124 MB), portable Windows package, draft release workflow for Linux and macOS.
- Byte-level Python reference builder, test vectors, cross-checks, fuzz targets, CI on Windows, Linux and macOS.

@@@ fuzzing
# Fuzz targets (NFR-03/5)

`cargo-fuzz` (libFuzzer) targets for every parser of `kapu-core`. The goal is
that no input, however broken, makes the library panic, overflow or read out
of bounds: every input must end in `Ok` or an `Err` with an FR-20 code.

| Target | What it feeds |
|---|---|
| `header` | any bytes into `Header::parse` |
| `sections` | whole file, strict and tolerant, tight limits, door reads |
| `docs` | `#DOCS` lines, unescape, comment stripping, served text |
| `catalog` | `#MAP`, `#DESC`, `#GRAPH` line parsers |
| `validate` | whole file, basic and full validation |
| `reader` | whole file, open every door plus unknown ids, describe, frame |

Run (nightly is needed only here; the seed corpus is the M1 test vectors):

```
mkdir fuzz/corpus/<target>; cp tests/vectors/*.kapu fuzz/corpus/<target>/
cargo +nightly fuzz run <target> fuzz/corpus/<target> -- -max_total_time=3600
```

Release gate: one hour per target without a finding.
`fuzz/corpus` and `fuzz/artifacts` are git-ignored.

@@@ overview
# KAPU overview

Your skills, code patterns, manuals and project notes go into one `.kapu` file. The agent first sees a map of every section with its token price, searches, and opens only the sections it needs. Nothing to host: one binary, an optional offline embedding model, and a plain UTF-8 file you can copy anywhere.

It is built for two cases: **coding agents** that should use your accumulated knowledge on any platform, and **local LLMs**, where every token in the context costs memory and seconds. Every reply of the MCP server starts with its own price in tokens and stays within a budget the agent sets.

## Try it in 30 seconds

**1. Install** the prebuilt `kapu` and `kapu-mcp` (Windows x64, Linux x64, macOS on Apple silicon), with the embedding model included. No Rust, no Python.

```bash
curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/piszabolcs/kapu/main/install.sh | sh
```

```powershell
irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/piszabolcs/kapu/main/install.ps1 | iex
```

The scripts download the archive of the latest release, check its SHA-256, unpack it under your user folder and print the next commands. You can also download the archive from [Releases](https://github.com/piszabolcs/kapu/releases) yourself.

**2. Pack** a folder of agent skills, or any folder of Markdown files (one room per file, `##` headings become sections):

```bash
kapu import --skills ~/.claude/skills skills.kapu
kapu import ./my-notes notes.kapu
```

**3. Connect** it to Claude Code (other clients: [Connect an agent](#connect-an-agent)):

```bash
claude mcp add kapu -- kapu-mcp
```

**4. Ask:** "Which of my skills fit this task? Look in `skills.kapu`." The agent reads the skill catalog in one reply and opens only what it needs.

To look around first, the archive has this project's own documentation as a KAPU file:

```bash
kapu map examples/kapu-docs.kapu
kapu search examples/kapu-docs.kapu "how do I connect it to an agent" --k 3
kapu open examples/kapu-docs.kapu overview/connect-an-agent
```

<details>
<summary>Build from source</summary>

With [Rust](https://rustup.rs) 1.90 or newer (any stable toolchain; CI tests with 1.99.1 and with the minimum):

```bash
git clone https://github.com/piszabolcs/kapu.git
cd kapu
cargo build --release -p kapu-cli -p kapu-mcp
```

Search by meaning is optional. For it, download the int8 ONNX form of [`intfloat/multilingual-e5-small`](https://huggingface.co/intfloat/multilingual-e5-small) (`onnx/model_qint8_avx512_vnni.onnx`, `onnx/tokenizer.json`, `onnx/config.json`) into `models/multilingual-e5-small-qint8` and convert it to the folder the tools look for: `python tools/onnx_to_kapu.py models/multilingual-e5-small-qint8 models/multilingual-e5-small-q8` (needs numpy). The release archives include the converted model already. Without the model, keyword search (BM25) still works.

</details>

The skill import keeps every skill's name as the title and its description as the first line, marks the room `kind: skill` (and `has_scripts: yes`), puts its scripts in as code blocks and its reference files in as linked rooms. A file built with the model carries its search vectors, so the first search on another machine needs no warm-up.

## Results

| Measured with Claude agents | Without KAPU | With KAPU |
|---|---|---|
| A 207,000-token manual the model had never seen, 12 questions | grep over the Markdown file: 8,280 tokens read per question, 12 correct | **4,104 tokens** per question, 12 correct |
| PLC manuals of six vendors, 36 questions | the model alone: 28% correct | **92% correct** (97% with the right page pasted in) |
| 24 questions those manuals do not answer | the model alone made up 11 answers | made up **1** |
| 91 agent skills, 12 development tasks | native Claude Code skills: $0.236 per task, 14,081 tokens fixed per session, 39 of 39 found | **$0.099** per task, **4,775** tokens fixed, 38 of 39 found |

Not every number favours KAPU: on public documentation with Stack Overflow questions the usual chunked hybrid search with the same embedding model is level with KAPU on the Rust book and ahead of it on MDN, and the native skills find one more skill than the KAPU file. The details, the method and every limit are below and on the **[results page](https://piszabolcs.github.io/kapu/results/)** ([source](docs/results/index.html); PDF in [English](docs/results/kapu-measurements-en.pdf) and [Hungarian](docs/results/kapu-measurements-hu.pdf)).

<details>
<summary>Full benchmarks and method</summary>

The same 12 questions about the PixelLab documentation (third-party, not published), the same prompt for every format, three blind judges checking every claim against the documentation (tools 0.1, format 1.1):

| How the agent got the knowledge | Tokens read per question (median) | Correct of 12 |
|---|---:|---:|
| **KAPU** (map, search, open) | **4,104** | **12** |
| One Markdown file, grep and line reads | 8,280 | 12 |
| The same text as a PDF, search and page reads | 8,207 | 11 |
| The whole file pasted into the context | 206,899 | - |

On public documentation with real Stack Overflow questions (format 1.3 with the search vectors in the file, one Windows 11 machine, release build; search is the median of `kapu bench`, opening a section the median over the MCP server):

| Corpus | Knowledge | `.kapu` file | Search | Open a section | Retrieval: KAPU 0.5 / 0.3 / chunks, BM25 / chunks, hybrid (0.3 model / e5) |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Rust book | 0.3 M tokens | 2.6 MB | 35 ms | 4 ms | 0.59 / 0.51 / 0.51 / 0.54 / 0.59 |
| Kubernetes docs | 3.9 M tokens | 31 MB | 48 ms | 8 ms | 0.58 / 0.45 / 0.48 / 0.47 / 0.53 |
| MDN Web Docs | 13.0 M tokens | 161 MB | 320 ms | 48 ms | 0.36 / 0.29 / 0.32 / 0.36 / 0.45 |

Retrieval is room-level recall@10 on the test part of the questions (60%, fixed by a hash): the page the accepted answer links to is among the first 10 hits. The chunk baselines cut every page into 512-token chunks and count a page once, the fair comparison with KAPU's room level; until 0.2.1 this table compared with chunks counted per chunk, which flattered KAPU. 0.5.0 replaces the static embedding model with `multilingual-e5-small`, run in plain Rust and stored in the file (4 bits a component): on the test part of seven question sets recall@10 +0.062 (95% interval +0.035 to +0.090) and MRR@10 +0.028 over 0.3 ([chapter 13](bench/RESULTS.md#13-plan-v3-a-contextual-model-in-plain-rust-050)). It does not pass the acceptance rule of 0.3.0: Hungarian questions on English skills lose MRR (0.49 to 0.44), and a small file is searched about 35 ms slower, for the query's vector; MDN is faster (408 to 320 ms). The trade was accepted. With the same model the chunked hybrid is level on the Rust book, behind KAPU on Kubernetes and ahead of it on MDN. Of the ranking changes measured for 0.3.0, only one hit per room passed the acceptance rule ([chapter 12](bench/RESULTS.md#12-plan-v2-phases-1-to-3-ablation-one-fix-that-holds-honest-baselines)). On a corpus of 244 skills and frontend notes with English questions it is 0.98, and `kapu_ask` opens the right room in its single reply for 96% of the questions.

**Knowledge a model does not have.** On public PLC manuals of six vendors (Beckhoff, Rexroth, Mitsubishi, Omron, Siemens, Emerson), Claude Sonnet answered:
- with the KAPU tools, **92%** correct;
- with the right page pasted into the prompt, 97%;
- with no knowledge file, 28%.

For questions the manuals do not answer, the KAPU agent said so for **23 of 24**; the model alone made an answer up for 11 of 24. Full results with charts, method and limits: **[results page](https://piszabolcs.github.io/kapu/results/)** ([source](docs/results/index.html); as PDF in [English](docs/results/kapu-measurements-en.pdf) and [Hungarian](docs/results/kapu-measurements-hu.pdf)) and [bench/RESULTS.md](bench/RESULTS.md); raw numbers in [bench/results/](bench/results/).

**Finding skills.** 12 development tasks with 39 relevant skills fixed in advance, Claude Opus, a prompt that asks for the skills ([chapter 11](bench/RESULTS.md#11-finding-skills-native-skills-one-kapu-file-a-markdown-folder)):

| 91 skills as | Relevant skills found | Wrong loaded | Tokens per task | $ per task | Fixed cost per session |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Native Claude Code skills | 39/39 | 0 | 44,953 | 0.236 | 14,081 |
| One KAPU file, with the catalog (0.2.1) | 38/39 | 3 | 48,973 | 0.099 | 4,775 |
| One KAPU file (0.2.0) | 38/39 | 0 | 88,418 | 0.255 | 4,774 |
| A folder of Markdown files | 39/39 | 12 | 107,726 | 0.336 | 5,430 |

With the catalog of tools 0.2.1 (`kapu_map kind=skill`: every skill with its description in one reply) the KAPU file finds as many skills as before in 5 calls instead of 9, with about the native skills' tokens; it costs less per task because it opens the first 2,000 tokens of each skill instead of the whole skill, and its fixed cost per session is a third of the native list's. The native skills still find one more and load nothing wrong.

What one session costs, whatever the size of the file: the six tools' descriptions and input schemas are 799 tokens, a map reply at most 1,000, ten search hits with an excerpt each at most 600 (format 1.2, measured on a 3,000-room file and the corpora above).

</details>

## How it works

A `.kapu` file is plain UTF-8 text. The Markdown body is the single source of truth; every other section is rebuilt from it, byte for byte, by `kapu build`.

```text
%KAPU 1.3          512-byte header: where each section starts, fingerprints, counts
#MAP    ... #END   groups and doors (rooms and their sections) with token prices and kinds
#DESC   ... #END   one-line descriptions and tags
#GRAPH  ... #END   links, shared rare terms and similar meaning between doors
#INDEX  ... #END   BM25 index; postings are positions in #DOCS, not ids
#DOCS   ... #END   byte offset, length, BLAKE3 fingerprint and word count of every door
#VEC    ... #END   every search vector, 4 bits a component, base64 (with a model only)
#BODY              the Markdown rooms, one after the other
```

- **Map first.** The map reply stays within 1,000 tokens even for 13 million tokens of documentation: the guide, a totals line and the groups (Leiden clusters named by their keywords); a group lists its rooms with prices.
- **Hybrid search.** BM25 with English and Hungarian stemming, plus a multilingual sentence encoder (`multilingual-e5-small`, int8) with one vector per window of up to 512 of its tokens, merged with reciprocal rank fusion. The encoder runs in plain Rust with exact integer products, so a vector has the same bits on every machine. The file keeps every vector at 4 bits a component (9-16% of the file), so a search reads them from the file and computes only the query's vector.
- **Opening is a seek.** The reader jumps to the byte offset in `#DOCS`, checks the section's fingerprint and returns its text. A section longer than the budget comes in parts, cut at headings or blank lines.
- **Edits stay safe.** If someone edits the body by hand, the fingerprints no longer match; the reader notices, rebuilds the positions from the room markers and reports a stale index until the next build.
- **Content scan.** Writing a room warns about text that tries to override an agent's instructions, hidden characters, long encoded blocks and unexpected download commands. It warns, it does not block.

The format is specified in [SPEC-KAPU.md](SPEC-KAPU.md) (the Hungarian original: [SPEC-KAPU.hu.md](SPEC-KAPU.hu.md)), with test vectors for every error code in [tests/vectors](tests/vectors) and a byte-level Python reference builder in [tools/build_vectors.py](tools/build_vectors.py).

## Desktop window

`kapu-gui` shows a file the way an agent sees it: map, rooms, search, graph and checks.

The release archives include it (`kapu-gui examples/kapu-docs.kapu`); from source: `cargo build --release -p kapu-gui`.

On Linux the window needs WebKitGTK: `sudo apt install libwebkit2gtk-4.1-dev libxdo-dev libssl-dev librsvg2-dev`.

## Why

AI agents get their working knowledge as Markdown: a manual, a specification, a library of skills, project notes. Pasting all of it costs tokens on every turn and pushes the useful part out of the context window. A vector database needs a server and an embedding service, cuts the text into chunks and loses the structure. Many small files lose the overview, and the agent has to guess which one to read.

KAPU keeps the Markdown as it is and adds, in the same file, a map of every section with its token price, a search index, the search vectors, a graph of related sections and fingerprints. The agent sees the table of contents first and pays only for what it opens.

| | A folder of Markdown | Vector database / RAG | KAPU |
|---|---|---|---|
| What you run | nothing | a database and an embedding service | one binary; the 135 MB embedding model is optional and offline |
| What the agent sees first | file names | nothing until it queries | a map with every section and its token price |
| What the agent reads | whole files, or grep lines without context | chunks cut at a fixed size | whole sections it picks, within a token budget |
| Structure | files and headings | lost in the chunks | rooms, sections, links, kinds and topic groups |
| One file to copy to another machine | no | no | yes, search vectors included |

## When not to use it

- **A small, well-split folder of Markdown.** An agent with `grep` and file reads works from few tokens there too; KAPU is not necessarily cheaper.
- **Team documentation that changes every day.** The derived sections change with every build, so a `git diff` of the `.kapu` file is noise. Keep the Markdown as the source in git and build the file from it (see [In git](#in-git)).
- **Skills that have to run.** KAPU gives the agent the knowledge of a skill, scripts included as text; it does not run them.

KAPU is strong where there are **many separate pieces of knowledge** (skills, patterns, projects), where **one large document** is the source, where **several agents or platforms** use the same knowledge, and where **the context is expensive** (local models).

## Connect an agent

The server speaks MCP over stdio; `KAPU_MODEL_DIR` points it at the model folder if it is not next to the binary.

**Claude Code** (tried):

```bash
claude mcp add kapu -- kapu-mcp
```

**Codex CLI**, in `~/.codex/config.toml` (not tried):

```toml
[mcp_servers.kapu]
command = "/path/to/kapu-mcp"
```

**VS Code, Copilot agent mode**, in `.vscode/mcp.json` (not tried):

```json
{ "servers": { "kapu": { "type": "stdio", "command": "/path/to/kapu-mcp" } } }
```

**A local model:** any MCP-capable client that runs a local model connects the same way. Give `kapu_ask` a `max_tokens` that fits the model's context. A measured setup is still to come.

Then ask, for example: "Use `notes.kapu` to answer: how do we deploy the staging server?" The server gives the agent six tools:

| Tool | What it returns |
|---|---|
| `kapu_map` | the map: groups, rooms and their token prices, the content language and kinds, the guide on first use; `kind=` gives a catalog of one kind (every skill with its description, in one reply) |
| `kapu_search` | the best sections for a query, each with its price and the line that matched; `kind=` filters |
| `kapu_ask` | search and open in one call: the start of the best section of each of the first 5 rooms, within the budget, and the other matches |
| `kapu_describe` | the description and tags of a section, without opening it |
| `kapu_open` | the text of sections, framed so it cannot pose as instructions; several in one call share the budget; long sections in parts |
| `kapu_write` | adds a room from Markdown (a project card, for example), rebuilds the file and reports what the content scan found |

Every reply starts with `reply: N tokens`, and every reading tool takes `max_tokens` (map 1,000, search 600, the others 2,000 by default); a reply never goes over it.

### Keep the agent on the tools

If the `.kapu` file is in the agent's working directory, the agent may read it directly instead of through the tools. Deny that:

- **Claude Code**, in the project's `.claude/settings.json` (Read rules also cover Grep and Glob):

  ```json
  { "permissions": { "deny": ["Read(**/*.kapu)"] } }
  ```

- **Codex CLI** and **VS Code** have no per-file read rule; a line in `AGENTS.md` or `.github/copilot-instructions.md` does the job: "Read `.kapu` files only through the kapu MCP tools."

A shell command can still print the file; the instruction line covers that case for every client.

## Project cards and kinds

A room can name its kind on a `kind:` line in its head: `skill`, `project`, `pattern` or `note`. The map and the search filter by it, and the map's totals line counts them (`kinds=project:4,skill:30`). A project card is a room for one project you did, at most about 600 tokens:

```bash
kapu template --kind project > card.md   # Goal, Period, Stack, Key decisions, What worked, What did not, Where the code is
kapu add notes.kapu card.md
kapu map notes.kapu --kind project
```

An agent can write one too: `kapu_write`'s description gives it the card's sections.

## In git

The body is the source; everything else in the file is rebuilt from it. Two ways to keep a `.kapu` file in git without noisy diffs:

```gitattributes
# .gitattributes: no line diffs for the derived file
*.kapu -diff
```

Or keep the Markdown in git (`kapu export notes.kapu ./notes`) and build the file from it (`kapu import ./notes notes.kapu`). `kapu` warns when a file is above 50 MiB, GitHub's warning size.

## Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  subgraph Input
    MD["Markdown files or skills"]
    MOD["multilingual-e5-small (int8, optional)"]
  end
  subgraph Core["kapu-core"]
    B["Builder: index, prices, graph, groups, vectors"]
    F[".kapu file"]
    R["Reader and validator"]
    S["Hybrid search (BM25 + window vectors, RRF)"]
  end
  subgraph Tools
    CLI["kapu (CLI)"]
    MCP["kapu-mcp (stdio)"]
    GUI["kapu-gui (Tauri)"]
  end
  AG["AI agent or local model"]
  MD --> B --> F --> R --> S
  MOD --> B
  MOD --> S
  R --> CLI & MCP & GUI
  S --> CLI & MCP & GUI
  MCP --> AG
```

| Layer | Technology |
|---|---|
| Language | Rust 1.90+ (edition 2024), no `unsafe` |
| File format | UTF-8 text, 512-byte header, BLAKE3 fingerprints, token prices with `tiktoken-rs` (o200k_base) |
| Search | BM25 with Snowball stemming (`rust-stemmers`), Model2Vec static embeddings (`tokenizers`, safetensors), sign-bit vectors with float rescoring, RRF |
| Graph and groups | `link`, `term` and `sim` edges, own Leiden implementation (checked against `leidenalg`), YAKE group names |
| Agent interface | MCP over stdio with `rmcp` |
| Command line and window | `clap` 4; Tauri 2 with plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript |
| Quality | clippy pedantic, cargo-deny, cargo-audit, cargo-fuzz, CI on Windows, Linux and macOS plus a minimum-Rust job, a byte-level Python reference builder |

## Project structure

```text
kapu/
├── crates/
│   ├── kapu-core/    format, builder, reader, validator, search, graph, groups, skill import
│   ├── kapu-cli/     the `kapu` command line tool
│   ├── kapu-mcp/     the `kapu-mcp` MCP server (stdio)
│   ├── kapu-gui/     the `kapu-gui` desktop window (Tauri, ui/ is the interface)
│   └── kapu-bench/   synthetic corpus generator and measurements
├── examples/         kapu-docs.kapu (this documentation), kapu-spec.kapu (the specification)
├── tests/            test vectors, one file per error code; older-format files; the tiny test model
├── bench/            RESULTS.md and the raw results as JSON
├── docs/             results page, logo, screenshots, demo
├── fuzz/             cargo-fuzz targets
├── tools/            Python reference builder, quantizer, measurement scripts
└── scripts/          check, fuzz and package scripts
```

## Status

KAPU is a working pre-release: the format, the command line, the MCP server and the Windows window are in daily use; the measurements above are a pilot, not a benchmark.

- [x] Prebuilt binaries for Windows, Linux and macOS from the release workflow (v0.2.0), one-line install scripts with checksums (v0.3.0)
- [ ] A local model tried end to end, with measurements (the Codex CLI and VS Code configurations above stay untried)
- [x] Finding skills: native skills, a KAPU file and a Markdown folder at 45 and 91 skills
- [x] Plan v2, phases 1 to 3: every ranking change measured on a fixed dev/test split, fair chunk baselines, oracle and abstention runs (0.3.0)
- [ ] Plan v2, phases 4 to 7: document and query expansion, smaller vector windows, a re-ranker, the filtered catalog, task runs with agents, repeated runs
- [ ] The same at 250 skills, and with a prompt that does not mention skills
- [x] The specification in English (v0.3.0)
- [ ] Better search when the question and the text are in different languages without translation (a Hungarian question on English text: 0.55 recall@10; translated by the agent: 0.78)
- [ ] The desktop window tested by hand on Linux and macOS

